A team computer's second bot never gets a screen without this.

`ensureExtraDisplayCommand` opens a lock file on fd 8, takes an flock, and then
starts Xvfb, the window manager, the browser, x11vnc and websockify in the
background. Those children inherit fd 8, and an flock lives on the open file
description rather than the process -- so the lock stays held for as long as any
of them runs, which is the whole life of the display. The next call to the same
function blocks on `flock 8` forever, and Rakazo's UI sits on
"Booting up ... computer". Measured: the shell's only child was `flock`, and an
independent `flock -w 3` on the same file returned BLOCKED.

The same script then runs `pkill -f 'novnc_proxy.*--listen <port>'`. That pattern
is unanchored, and the running shell's own command line CONTAINS it verbatim, so
the script signals itself and dies partway -- after Xvfb and the browser are up,
before x11vnc and websockify. That is why the display existed while its listeners
never appeared, and why the lock was orphaned in the first place.

Closing fd 8 in each background child fixes the deadlock. Bracketing one
character of the pkill pattern stops the regex matching the text of the regex.
Neither is specific to this application: both are generic shell defects.

